From 1cd3b814d9e3e8b866072033e7fec5dc650a63f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: moritztng <moritz.thuening@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 05:13:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [matmul] 1D mcast_in1: issue the output write from the in0
 RISC

In the 1D mcast_in1 matmul the in1 sender/receiver kernel does two jobs on one data movement
RISC: it feeds the in1 multicast and it writes the output block out of the CB. The in0 sender
kernel runs on the other data movement RISC and is idle for the whole write, so the op floors at
the serial sum of the two. Moving the write to the in0 RISC lets them overlap.

The write itself is unchanged. matmul_out_writer_in0.hpp is the writer body lifted out of
reader_bmm_tile_layout_in1_receiver_writer_padding.cpp: same subblock loops, same barrier per
subblock, same padding handling, same compile time args in the same order. Only the thread that
issues it changes, so the output is bit-identical, not just close.

It applies where the two threads provably do the same work: interleaved in0 and output, no fused
op, no bias, tilized output, and not reuse_in0_in_CB (under which the in1 writer walks in1_B
batches while the in0 sender walks in0_B). Everything else keeps the shipped program.

Measured on a Blackhole p150a at a forced, sampled 1350 MHz, arms interleaved in one process with
an A/A twin for the floor:

  per call, b=16 M=512 N=512, 11x10 grid, HiFi4, fp32_dest_acc_en, packer_l1_acc
  K=128   0.06197 ms -> 0.04247 ms   1.459x
  K=1024  0.14730 ms -> 0.13061 ms   1.128x
  A/A floor on the shipped arm: 0.312 %

19.5 us saved at K=128 and 16.7 us at K=1024: the same absolute saving across an 8x change in K,
which is the K-independent output write leaving the serial path. Bit-exactness was checked with
torch.equal against the shipped program over seven shapes covering the H and W padding tails, a
1x1 grid where the mcast sender core is the whole output, and batch > 1 with fuse_batch off:
7/7 identical, max abs diff 0.
